<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tartus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tortosa, Antaradus, Tortosa (Syria)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartus</a> is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, facing the island of Arwad. As Antaradus it was a mainland settlement of Arwad in antiquity, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> stronghold held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, whose cathedral of Our Lady survives. It is now Syria's second port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tartus.jpg" alt="Tartus" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taras Kalapun: xslim (Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boats_on_Tartus_boat_harbor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartus</a> lies on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, on a coastal plain bordered to the east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-coastal-mountain-range">Syrian Coastal Mountain Range</a>, whose limestone hills in places give way to basalt. Arwad, the only inhabited island on the Syrian coast, lies a few kilometres off the shore. The name derives from the Ancient Greek Antaradus, meaning the town facing Aradus, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Ṭarṭūs in Arabic. It has a Mediterranean climate with mild, wet winters and hot, humid summers. The city was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a> until the 1970s, when Tartus Governorate was created.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartus</a> was founded as a Phoenician colony of Aradus, the larger settlement on the island, and was called Antaradus. Little remains of it. In the Greco-Roman and Byzantine periods it had its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and bishops of Antaradus and of Aradus appear at councils from Chalcedon in 451 onward. The first chapel dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> is said to have been built there in the 3rd century, and the city was favoured by Constantine for its devotion to her cult. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> armies under Ubadah ibn al-Samit conquered it in 636, and Mu'awiya I built an amsar complex there.</p><p>Frankish forces captured the city in 1099 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. Raymond of Saint-Gilles recaptured it in February 1102 after a two-week siege, and in 1123 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> built the semi-fortified Cathedral of Our Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> over a Byzantine church popular with pilgrims.</p><p>In 1152 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> was handed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, who made it a military headquarters and about 1165 built a castle with a large chapel, an elaborate keep and thick double concentric walls. Nur ad-Din Zangi held it briefly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> recaptured it in 1188, but some Templars kept the keep for another hundred years until it fell in 1291, the last Templar outpost on the Syrian mainland. They then held the island of Arwad for another decade. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> the city lost its prestige, which it regained under Ottoman rule through trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartus</a> in 1832. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> regained it in 1839, and in 1840 British frigates and marines attacked the citadel without capturing it. Ottoman rule lasted until 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartus</a> is a major port and the second largest port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a>, with a 2023 population estimated at 458,327. The historic centre is built on and inside the walls of the Templar fortress, whose moat still separates the old town from the modern city. The former cathedral, the Romanesque-Gothic Notre-Dame of Tartus of the 12th century, has housed the National Museum of Tartus since 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tartus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ramon Berenguer I extended Barcelona’s authority in the eleventh century, and Ramon Berenguer IV conquered <b>Tortosa</b> in 1148 and Lleida in 1149, completing what became known as New Catalonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The young commune also fought for the western Mediterranean, joining Alfonso VII of Castile against Almería and Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona against <b>Tortosa</b> in 1146–1148.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Arwad occupies a small island off <b>Tartus</b>, giving it a sheltered harbour and natural defences.</blockquote><a href="/state/arwad">Arwad</a></li><li><blockquote>The county ran along the coast from Jubail to <b>Tortosa</b> and inland to the Orontes.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a></li></ul></section>
